  "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2033}-5y} & =L{-2{u}_{3}(t)} \\\\ L{{y}^{\u2033}}-5L{y} & =-2L{{u}_{3}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L{y}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{\u2033}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{\u2033}}={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{\u2032}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{\u2032}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{\u2033}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-s+2.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{u}_{3}(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2033}}-5L{y} & =-2L{{u}_{3}(t)} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-s+2)-5Y(s) & =-2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-5)Y(s) & =s-2-\\frac{2{e}^{-3s}}{s} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-5)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-2s-2{e}^{-3s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-2s-2{e}^{-3s}}{s({s}^{2}-5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
